// Package keycustody holds the keys that encrypt retained copies, and never
// shows one.
//
// # What this is
//
// EPIC N (#967) makes encryption a property of a stored copy. Before any
// copy can be encrypted, something has to own the key, and this package
// is that something (FR-63, FR-64, FR-66; issue #990). It answers three
// questions and refuses a fourth:
//
//   - where a MANAGED key lives: generated by the format ADR 0027 selects (an age X25519 identity,
//     drawn from crypto/rand), written once
//     as a mode-0600 file in a 0700 directory beside config.yaml, and read
//     back through core/internal/secretref so the custody rule that package
//     enforces (owner-only mode, no symlinked final component, no group- or
//     world-writable ancestor) is the rule applied here, by the same code,
//     rather than a second copy of it;
//   - whether an EXTERNAL key resolves: an operator declares one in
//     secretref.Ref's shape and nothing else (file, env, command; exactly
//     one), and the probe reports resolves / declared-but-unreachable /
//     not-declared without ever holding the material longer than the probe;
//   - whether the deployment could RECOVER: a disaster-recovery bundle is
//     the one deliberate moment managed material leaves the host, and until
//     a person has confirmed they hold one, "encryption on, no bundle" is a
//     durable degraded fact in health.Report.KeyCustody.
//
// The fourth question, "what is the key", has no answer on any surface
// after that export. No method returns existing material as a value: the
// only door is a callback (Store.WithKey, WithExternalKey) whose argument is
// zeroed when it returns, and every exported shape a route or a CLI verb
// could render is classified in surfaces_test.go as carrying none, with the
// Bundle the single listed exception.
//
// # The honest sentence about the threat model
//
// apps/common/auth/local/secrets.go already argued it for an SMTP password
// and it holds here unchanged: there is no key this process could hold that
// an attacker able to read a 0600 file in the state directory could not
// also read. A managed key file beside the journal does not protect the
// copies from whoever owns this host. What it protects them from is whoever
// owns the BUCKET: the off-site copy is unreadable to the object store's
// operator, and that is the whole of the claim. The DR bundle is the only
// thing that changes the threat model, because it moves the key off the
// host, which is why exporting it is explicit, recorded, and the thing a
// person must confirm before the degraded state clears.
//
// # What is decided elsewhere
//
// The format is ADR 0027's (#989): age v1 through filippo.io/age, with an
// X25519 identity as the key. This package sees it through one small
// interface, Format (format.go), implemented by AgeFormat (age.go): generate
// a key, derive its recipient, and open a stream, so a bundle can decrypt a
// copy on a clean machine. This package designs no cryptography and never
// encrypts; the transform is #991's. The registry of key ids, recipients,
// timestamps, statuses, exports and confirmations is durable state behind
// the Registry interface; FileRegistry here is the durable implementation,
// and a journal-backed one over migration 0015's encryption_keys table is
// not written: that table's status check allows only active and retired and
// it has no table for exports, so mapping this package's lifecycle onto it
// needs a schema addition that belongs to the migration's owner. Until
// then the sidecar and the catalog cannot answer "which key does this copy
// need" from one place.
//
// Retirement and the refusal to retire a key that still guards an ACTIVE
// placement are #992's: KeyRecord carries the Retired timestamp and the
// status vocabulary for it, and nothing here moves a key to retired.
package keycustody
